analysisWe need to read the Java code and produce an assert statement that matches the comment starting with "@@@". The comment is:

```
// @@@ If the list initially contains the specified element, then the result of the removal operation is true.
```

We are inside method:

```java
public boolean removeFirstOccurrence(Object o) {
    return remove(o);
    // @@@ If the list initially contains the specified element, then the result of the removal operation is true.
}
```

We need to output an assert statement that expresses: If the list initially contains the specified element, then the result (return value) of the removal operation is true.

We need to use \old to refer to the list before removal. The method returns boolean, accessible as \result. The condition "list initially contains the specified element" can be expressed as \old(this.contains(o)). Since contains is a public observer method defined earlier. So the assertion: \old(this.contains(o)) => \result == true. Or simply \old(this.contains(o)) => \result.

Because \result is boolean, we can just assert: \old(this.contains(o)) => \result. The syntax: assert \old(this.contains(o)) => \result;.

Make sure to use parentheses if needed: but => lower precedence than && and ||, but no other operators. So fine.

Thus final answer:

<code>assert \old(this.contains(o)) => \result;</code>

